#!/usr/bin/python3
"""a7a-gpu-gap: what this GPU can do (proven by the vendor DDK) against what the open stack exposes, turned into a
ranked, actionable list. docs/GPU-GAP-2026-09-10.md is its report; the snapshots it compares live in docs/gpu-caps/.

  a7a-gpu-gap snapshot [--out FILE] [--bench] [--bench-full]   ON THE BOARD: record the booted stack
  a7a-gpu-gap report --open FILE --vendor FILE [--out MD]       diff two snapshots, evaluate Mesa's version gates
  a7a-gpu-gap gates --mesa-src DIR [--out FILE]                 extract the gates from a Mesa source tree

snapshot writes one JSON file for the stack that is booted, with one entry per "view" (a way of running GL and
Vulkan clients on that boot):
  open stack (drm/imagination 'powervr' bound)  open         Mesa's PowerVR Vulkan driver + zink: what every app gets
  vendor boot (pvrsrvkm bound)                  ddk          the DDK under /opt/pvr (pvr-run's environment)
                                                fedora-mesa  Fedora's Mesa on the same boot, for reference (llvmpipe)
                                                zink-on-ddk  Fedora's zink over the DDK's Vulkan ICD: the GL version zink
                                                             reaches on the Vulkan features this GPU is proven to have
Each view records vulkaninfo --json (features, limits, extensions, formats), eglinfo (every platform), glxinfo -l
(when a desktop session is logged in) and a small EGL probe (this script's hidden `probe` mode, ctypes, surfaceless)
that reads the GL integer limits Mesa's gates compare. The probe runs a second time with MESA_GL_VERSION_OVERRIDE=
4.6COMPAT / MESA_GLES_VERSION_OVERRIDE=3.2 in its own environment ONLY to make Mesa list every extension flag the
driver computed (a GL 2.1 context hides extensions that need GL 3.1+): a diagnostic read of Mesa's internal state,
labelled as such in the snapshot and the report; no application ever runs with an override. --bench adds
glmark2-es2 (the same sizes on both stacks) with thermal sampling; it needs the logged-in desktop.

report evaluates the version gates of Mesa (src/mesa/main/version.c, compute_version and compute_version_es2,
extracted verbatim by `gates` into a7a-gpu-gap-mesa-<ver>-gates.json) against the flags and limits the open stack
really has, names every failing term, maps it to the Vulkan feature, extension or limit behind it (GL_FLAG_NEEDS
below, with the Mesa source lines), checks the vendor snapshot for that item, and ranks the fixes by what they
unlock (APPS below). Nothing here writes to the system: snapshot only reads and runs the info tools.
"""
